WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.896
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:09.420
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.611
Unit two: Elections & Voting. Today you will emphasise election facts with negative inversion.

4
00:00:15.911 --> 00:00:18.482
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:22.457
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.317 --> 00:00:24.167
Ballot.

7
00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:29.354
Ballot means a paper or screen on which a person records their vote.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.693
Each citizen casts one ballot in secret.

9
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:35.669
Your turn. Say: Ballot.

10
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:39.395
Ballot.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.047
Candidate.

12
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.002
Candidate means a person who is trying to be elected.

13
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:48.603
The candidate promised to improve public transport.

14
00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:52.008
Your turn. Say: Candidate.

15
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:55.798
Candidate.

16
00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.551
Campaign.

17
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.978
Campaign means a series of activities aimed at winning an election.

18
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:05.047
The campaign lasted six exhausting months.

19
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.128
Your turn. Say: Campaign.

20
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:11.933
Campaign.

21
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:13.951
Voter turnout.

22
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.830
Voter turnout means the percentage of eligible people who actually vote.

23
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:22.368
Voter turnout was highest among older citizens.

24
00:01:22.983 --> 00:01:25.624
Your turn. Say: Voter turnout.

25
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:29.700
Voter turnout.

26
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.834
Electoral system.

27
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.579
Electoral system means the rules that decide how votes become seats.

28
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:39.775
Each electoral system favours different parties.

29
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:43.547
Your turn. Say: Electoral system.

30
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.947
Electoral system.

31
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:49.928
Polling station.

32
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:53.833
Polling station means a place where people go to vote.

33
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.042
The polling station opened at seven in the morning.

34
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.476
Your turn. Say: Polling station.

35
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:04.625
Polling station.

36
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:06.431
Referendum.

37
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:11.483
Referendum means a vote by all citizens on one specific question.

38
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:15.023
The referendum asked whether to change the constitution.

39
00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:18.037
Your turn. Say: Referendum.

40
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.052
Referendum.

41
00:02:22.817 --> 00:02:24.004
Political party.

42
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:28.758
Political party means an organised group that competes in elections.

43
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.592
A political party must explain its policies clearly.

44
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:36.254
Your turn. Say: Political party.

45
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.310
Political party.

46
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.937
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.783 --> 00:02:51.826
Visal, a journalist writes 'Rarely has voter turnout been so low.' Why is 'has' before 'turnout'?

48
00:02:52.917 --> 00:03:04.102
That is negative inversion. After a negative adverb such as rarely, never, hardly or not until, the auxiliary comes before the subject, for strong emphasis.

49
00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:07.077
Can you give an election example?

50
00:03:08.183 --> 00:03:21.566
Not until the ballots were counted did the candidate admit defeat. Only when turnout rose did the campaign feel successful. Hardly had the polling station opened when queues formed.

51
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.278
Is it common in speech?

52
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:33.643
No, mainly in formal writing and speeches. Rarely does a referendum attract as many voters as a general election.

53
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.254
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:42.576
Grammar focus: Negative inversion: Not until and Hardly had and Rarely has.

55
00:03:42.876 --> 00:03:44.098
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.783 --> 00:03:48.234
Not until the ballots were counted did the candidate admit defeat.

57
00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.496
Only when turnout rose did the campaign feel successful.

58
00:03:53.383 --> 00:03:56.881
Hardly had the polling station opened when queues began to form.

59
00:03:57.833 --> 00:04:06.242
After rarely, never, hardly, not until or only when, put the auxiliary before the subject: Not until... did he... Use it for emphasis in formal writing.

60
00:04:06.983 --> 00:04:10.855
Be careful! Don't say: Hardly he had arrived when it rang.

61
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.821
Say: Hardly had he arrived when it rang.

62
00:04:16.121 --> 00:04:18.481
Invert the auxiliary after hardly.

63
00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:21.617
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.433 --> 00:04:23.922
Why Every Vote Counts.

65
00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:27.789
Elections are the clearest way for citizens to hold power to account.

66
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.669
Rarely has a campaign succeeded without a strong political party behind it.

67
00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:38.046
Not until voter turnout rises can an electoral system claim real legitimacy.

68
00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:42.902
Only when people believe their ballot matters do they walk to the polling station.

69
00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.999
Even a referendum, which asks one simple question, requires careful public debate.

70
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.555
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.417 --> 00:04:57.556
Never blank turnout been so high.

72
00:04:57.856 --> 00:05:00.905
A, has. B, does. C, is.

73
00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:06.385
The answer is A: has.

74
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:11.012
A vote on a single question put to all citizens is a blank.

75
00:05:11.312 --> 00:05:15.511
A, campaign. B, candidate. C, referendum.

76
00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.426
The answer is C: referendum.

77
00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:24.455
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:24.755 --> 00:05:31.508
A, Hardly he had arrived when it rang.. B, Hardly had he arrived when it rang.. C, Hardly he arrived when it rang..

79
00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:38.587
The answer is B: Hardly had he arrived when it rang..

80
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.342
The place where people cast their votes is a blank.

81
00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:47.468
A, polling station. B, political party. C, legislature.

82
00:05:51.033 --> 00:05:53.323
The answer is A: polling station.

83
00:05:54.483 --> 00:05:57.783
According to the reading, people vote when they believe their ballot…

84
00:05:58.083 --> 00:06:01.934
A, is free. B, matters. C, is secret.

85
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.453
The answer is B: matters.

86
00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:12.120
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:12.983 --> 00:06:15.343
Why does voter turnout matter in an election?

88
00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.708
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:26.008 --> 00:06:50.107
Voter turnout matters because an election result is only as legitimate as the share of citizens who took part. When turnout is low, the winners may represent a small minority, and many groups may feel ignored. High turnout shows public trust in the system and gives the government a stronger mandate to act on behalf of everyone.

90
00:06:50.407 --> 00:06:52.256
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:53.150 --> 00:07:00.052
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and negative inversion: not until and hardly had and rarely has.

92
00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:06.359
Today's value is civic responsibility. Take care of your city, because it is everyone's home.

93
00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:08.905
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:09.205 --> 00:07:11.811
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

